  "slug": "connectivity-assignment-fd91",
  "summary": "Image-based models now predict molecular, spatial, and developmental endpoints from morphology across spatial transcriptomics, organoids, and bone.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is morphology serving as the input or bridge to molecular and spatial inference. RNA2seg integrates RNA point clouds with membrane and nuclear stains for cell segmentation in imaging-based spatial transcriptomics, while stGCL and SpaConTDS fuse expression, coordinates, and histology through contrastive learning to identify spatial domains. Other items apply morphology-based deep learning to different endpoints: a retinal organoid pipeline predicts RPE and lens outcomes from time-lapse imaging, an Attention U-Net segments osteocyte networks that distinguish young from aged mouse bone, and SurfAge-Net predicts regional brain age on spherical cortical surfaces. What is new is the combination of image morphology, graph structure, and contrastive or segmentation learning, with prediction running from image toward molecular or spatial state in several cases.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-31"
}
